From: DKR-R Windows 95 port
Subject: [PATCH] Name the thread that ends each silence, and the one that earned each microsecond

Patch 0042 measures the wall time on which no guest thread runs and charges
half of it to the renderer. The other half -- a sixth of every second at the
title screen -- has no name.

The thread that wakes first is the closest thing to one the scheduler can offer
without instrumenting every wake site, and it separates the candidates that
matter: the audio thread ending the waits says the audio path paces the frame,
the game thread ending them says it is the vertical interval or a message
queue.

Sixteen slots by guest thread id, everything above folded into the last, and
only the interval's *end* recorded -- asking which thread went to sleep last
would name the one that had least to do, not the one being waited for.

  [trace][waker] t1=NNN us/NN t3=NNN us/NN


The same hook also splits `guest-run` itself by thread, which costs nothing and
answers the question the frame's largest remaining item raises. 500 display
lists, a frame of 122.5 ms:

  id 5  the scheduler       38.80 ms  31.7%   the RDRAM snapshot is inside it
  id 1  the idle thread     17.23 ms  14.1%
  id 3  the game thread     15.32 ms  12.5%
  id 4  the audio manager    2.11 ms   1.7%

The recompiled game is an eighth of its own frame, and libultra's idle thread
costs more than it does -- subject to the caveat that "not blocked" is not
"executing", which this instrument cannot separate.
---
--- a/ultramodern/src/threads.cpp
+++ b/ultramodern/src/threads.cpp
